// the arguments passed to a function are called parameters. They are local variables that exist only within the scope of the function. Once the function finishes executing, the parameters are destroyed and cannot be accessed outside of the function.

// this means the arguments are the copy variables of the original variables passed to the function. Any changes made to the parameters within the function do not affect the original variables outside the function. This is known as pass-by-value.

// scope of variables refers to the region of the program where a variable is defined and can be accessed. In C++, variables can have different scopes, such as global scope, local scope, and block scope.

